knowledge-base/how-to/alert-runbooks.md:35:| `OutboxParkedHigh` | Events parked >15m on unresolvable subject/payload refs. | Are the referenced entities landing? Reader lag, or a feed gap? | Usually the referenced entity has not been ingested yet — check the data-plane poller and its watermark. Parking is correct behaviour; the alert is about how LONG. |
knowledge-base/how-to/alert-runbooks.md:159:| `DocExpiryNextAssignmentIncomplete` | A doc-expiry row's crew is rostered onto an uncancelled flight the platform cannot place in time or name (T6.6). The column shows "unknown" rather than a guessed time. | Read `bff_doc_expiry_next_assignment_total` by `reason` FIRST — `assignment_incomplete` is the only reason alarmed, and the three unalarmed ones (`resolver_not_wired`, `no_crew_ref`, `roster_never_synced`) tell you whether the roster is populated here at all. Then: are `CrewDutySegment` rows landing with `scheduledDeparture`, `dep` and `arr`? | Fix the ingestion that drops the clock or the stations. **Do NOT close this by falling back to some other time on the row** — a guessed next assignment is exactly what the unresolved state exists to refuse, and an operator deciding whether an expiring licence is urgent would be acting on it. The three unalarmed reasons are counted deliberately: each is true by construction while `CrewDutySegment` is C10-gated, and an alarm on them would fire from boot and stay saturated (LS-21). Arm `roster_never_synced` on the first cell whose duty sync has run. |
knowledge-base/how-to/alert-runbooks.md:180:| `SwapAcceptsStale` | More than three swap accepts in an hour were refused 409 `SWAP_PLAN_STALE` (`bff_swap_accept_check_total{outcome="stale"}`): the plan named was no longer the one the board serves at that rank. One refusal is normal (two controllers on one case, a recompute landing mid-decision); a run means people act on boards that change under them. | Is `SwapRecomputeFailing` also firing? Does the console recompute a stale board on open (`POST /api/tasks/:id/swap` with `trigger: "tab-open"` in the access log)? Is the frame changing every five-minute bucket because the roster feed rewrites rows it did not change? | Fix the recompute or the churning feed. Never relax the check: a verdict naming a plan nobody was shown is the failure it exists to prevent. Nothing was recorded on a refusal. See [crew-swap](../features/crew-swap.md) §5. |
knowledge-base/how-to/alert-runbooks.md:205:| `AeroApiOverlayUnresolved` | Stored AeroAPI rows existed for a flight-status read but the ident→leg join (`buildAeroOverlay`) matched NONE of them to a canonical `FlightLeg` (T6.7b, at the BFF). | `BFF_AEROAPI_CARRIER_MAP` FIRST — an absent or wrong ICAO→IATA entry silently drops every ident for that carrier, by design (never a mis-join). Then whether the poll job is even landing rows for this tenant/date at all. | The estimate overlay is contributing nothing this read; every leg's estimate stays AIMS-only, quietly. Fix the carrier map before suspecting the AeroAPI feed itself — a wrong or missing mapping is the far more common cause than the feed being empty. |
knowledge-base/how-to/alert-runbooks.md:228:| `AbsenceReportPending` | The oldest sick-call report item has been `pending` more than 5 minutes (`crew_absence_report_pending_oldest_seconds` above 300; critical): its line has not been stored, so no `crew.absence_reported` event has gone out. | `stratum.sync_feed_items`, kind `report`, class `pending`: the item's key is the line's source id and `item_date` its day; whether the crew-day was read (the roster-linked feed tick log and `canonical_feed_*` series, or the N-OC cycle); the producer's refusals for the key; `subject_kind = 'crew_source'` on the item (its `subject_id` is `<system>:<crew source id>`) means the crew member is not in the store yet, so check the crew sync (`CrewMember`, tier T2) and the crew's presence in the source. | Fix whatever stops the read (a stalled feed, an exhausted budget, a refused line) or the crew sync; a crew who lands is bound by the next 10 s consumer pass and its crew-day read at once (at most 25 crew-days per pass, deferred while the `scoped_p1` breaker refuses, retried each pass until the line lands). The page's age counts from the bind, not from the crew's wait. Outcome counters: a rising `awaiting_crew` with a flat `crew_bound` means the crew sync is not landing crew; `release_failed` means the release read threw or timed out; `dropped_erased` and `skipped_not_ours` are items dropped silently at the bind. At `sickCall.landWithinSeconds` (600 s) the item closes `unlandable{timeout}` and `AbsenceReportUnlandable` takes over; a line that lands later is still routed. |
