tools/canonical-model/model/unified-model-0.11.md:82:**Emission.** Ruling 0.10-1. A LOG_ROSTER insert whose code the mapping classifies as absence, whose crew is in the absence population and whose LEG_DAY is in [today − sickCall.lookbackDays, today + horizon] writes an absence_report item (stratum.sync_feed_items kind absence_report, class pending, keyed by the line's source id, with the clear columns source_log_id = TABLELOGID, source_logged_at = the log time and policy_lock_hash = the compiled mapping's lock hash, and subject_id; no classification is stored: the consumer recomputes it from the landed line's code, ruling 0.11-3) and schedules the crew-day's scoped re-read at priority 1. When the line is stored, the absence_report consumer writes its event patch (reportedAt, reportWatermark, and reportedClassification recomputed from the line's code under the current lock; a lock hash that differs from the item's is counted absence_report_policy_changed), then ingests crew.absence_reported into the outbox and marks the item emitted; a crash between them replays the emission, deduped by the deterministic dedupKey. A log delete of an emitted item's line emits crew.absence_retracted and marks the item retracted; a delete before emission marks it retracted and emits nothing. An insert older than the lookback is baseline, counted absence_reported_retroactive. Every other write is baseline: the rotation, the backfill, a re-key, a mapping edit, the synthetic seed. Natural key: logroster:{crew}:{legDay}:{code}, unchanged (domains/airline/crew/adapters/src/aims/reader.ts:1522). Watermark: the log row's TABLELOGID, unchanged, so the dedupKey stays byte-equal with the legacy sick-call bridge's (platform/contracts/src/dedupKey.ts, CR008-B3). Payload: {crewMemberRef, code, classification, interval, affectedDutyRefs: []}, unchanged (affectedDutyRefs stays [] on the wire for byte-equality with the legacy bridge, ruling 0.11-2); classification is absence.reportedClassification, the one the routing reads (lens 1 0.10 minor: it had no reader); interval is the UTC LEG_DAY [00:00Z, next 00:00Z) (ruling 0.10-3). occurredAt: the log row's own time (ONDAY + ONMIN); a difference from detectedAt beyond 60 minutes (below every non-zero base offset at Copa and Porter) is counted reported_at_skew with its reason, so a wrong zone shows (lens 1 m-f). Outbox: the event is ingested after the line and its event patch are stored; a crash replays it from the pending item, deduped by the dedupKey. Lifecycle: pending → emitted → retracted, or pending → unlandable{reason}: out_of_population, out_of_window, refused, base_pending (the crew has no base), deleted_before_landing (→ retracted). An item still pending after T_land (tenants/<id>/sync.json sickCall.landWithinSeconds, default 300) is unlandable{timeout}. unlandable is critical (absence_report_unlandable): a sick call the platform could not route, for the operator to handle by hand. Items carry subject_id (a CHECK requires it, §6.7), so erasure deletes them (no erased state: a deleted item has no state; dba 0.10 info); an emitted item is kept while its sick-call task is open (the router and the reconcile resolve through it), then expires with the window.. Routing: the router, the demand reconcile and the review queue resolve the absence line through the item (natural key → the emitted item → the line's source id), not by rewriting the natural key (domains/airline/crew/workflows/src/sickCallRouting.ts:17-19 today maps logroster: to absence:). Two code rows of one crew, day and code: the natural key is shared, so the most recently emitted item wins and the task stays one task. A legacy-bridge event that arrives before the item is emitted is parked by the router through the outbox's parked state (OutboxParkedHigh) and resolved when the item is emitted, never thrown (lens 2 B1).. Line key: SourceRosterChange gains LEG_DEP, LEG_CARRIER and LEG_LEGCD for code rows (LOG_ROSTER holds them, domains/airline/crew/adapters/src/aims/mssqlDb.ts:1861-1862; the port's legId is flights only, domains/airline/crew/adapters/src/port.ts:115-129), so the item addresses its line's source id exactly (lens 1 N-M2, lens 2 B1); the adapter-sdk port inventory moves with it. Lag reset: a LOG_ROSTER lag reset replays the skipped range's mapped-absence inserts and deletes in bounded chunks with a durable replay cursor, each chunk reserved and settled at priority 1; the feed cursor is held until the replay completes (lag_reset_replay_incomplete, critical, while it does not); frozen-day corrections are batched per crew into one scoped read; a RosterActivity lag reset re-stales live days only (lens 2 M14). Mapping edits: a mapping edit emits nothing (apps/bff/src/server.ts:779-791 refuses auto-retraction on purpose). The store re-derives category and classification of the stored lines whose code's mapping changed, with no AIMS read (a re-derive item per code, counted roster_activity_recategorised); a reported line that leaves absence is recorded in the operator ledger (reported_absence_code_unmapped), which only an operator clears (lens 1 N-M3, lens 2 M6). Reset: the reset carries the LOG_ROSTER cursor across: resetData reads the cursor row before the TRUNCATE and re-inserts it in the same transaction, so the cursor resumes where the stopped services left it; the inserts of the maintenance window are replayed as signals, bounded by the lookback, counted absence_signals_replayed_at_reset (lens 2 M2). The rotation's and backfill's writes stay baseline: no sick-call storm. Bridge: the legacy bridge reads the compiled activity mapping instead of DP_ABSENCE_CODES and applies the same lookback bound (an older absenceDate is not emitted, counted absence_reported_retroactive) (lens 1 N-m4); a red test holds the Sense path and the bridge byte-equal on dedupKey AND equal on payload, a back-entered case included.
