tools/canonical-model/model/unified-model-0.6.md:659:| P11 | 16,000 legs: ADATE = DAY + (STA >= 1440) on 10,464; ADATE = DAY − 1 on 2,280; ADATE < 1000 (garbage) on 588. Against the 0.5 distribution of the same window (ADATE − DAY: 0 on 13,130, −1 on 2,280; scratchpad/unified-model-probe-past.out:10) the 10,464 are exactly the ADATE = DAY legs with STA < 1440: the 2,666 legs that land on the NEXT UTC day still carry ADATE = DAY, and ADATE is never DAY + 1. The −1 group spreads over STD hours 00–22 UTC. So ADATE is NOT the UTC arrival day and not a departure-local day; it is always on or before DAY — consistent with the date of the aircraft rotation (AROUTE) the leg belongs to (not proven). | `scratchpad/unified/probes-0.6.out:56-104` | FlightLeg.scheduled.arrival: DAY + STA (pre-wrapped); ADATE is not an arrival day and is never read<br>FlightLeg.localDepartureDate: computed from scheduled.departure (DAY + STD, UTC minutes) in the origin station's tz; never from ADATE<br>FlightLeg.operationalDate: the 'ADATE semantics open' note closed. The habit of reading ADATE as the landing day is NOT confirmed — it is refuted on 2,666 of 16,000 legs |
