tools/canonical-model/reviews/0.10/critic-0.10-lens1.md:46:| N10-M3. `unlandable{timeout}` is terminal at the latency alert's own threshold, and a re-entered sick call has no transition | MAJOR | T_land defaults to 300 s, after which the item is `unlandable{timeout}`: terminal, critical, "handle by hand" (page:1897). `AbsenceReportPending` pages "above 300 s" over pending items only, and unlandable items are excluded (page:4909). A pending item cannot be older than 300 s, so that alert can never fire: a dead rule (E2). A line that lands at 301 s is never emitted. That happens under a scoped re-read breaker in half-open backoff (1, 5, 15 min, page:4091) or one budget deferral. Ruling 0.10-1 closes the item only for "a line that cannot land", and a late line can land. **Re-entry:** a sick call deleted and entered again on the same line key collides on the item's primary key `(tenant_id, feed, entity, kind, key)` (syncFeed/postgres.ts:48). `retracted` is terminal, so the new TABLELOGID's report is lost or silently merged. | Make the timeout non-terminal: `late` (critical, paged) still emits when the line lands. Keep `unlandable` for structural reasons only (out of population or window, refused, base_pending, erased). Define `retracted → pending` on a new insert with a new TABLELOGID, giving a new dedupKey and a child task under recurrence `mint` (sickCallRouting.ts:86). Red tests: a line landing at T_land + 1 cycle is emitted once; delete-then-re-enter yields two reports. |
tools/canonical-model/reviews/0.10/critic-0.10-lens1.md:63:- 0.10-1 is honoured in intent (emit after landing), but not soundly in three places: the item cannot store its data (N10-B1); a late line is declared unlandable at the same moment the latency alert would fire (N10-M3); and the resolver and park it relies on do not exist (N10-M2).
